{"created": "2026-03-25T03:05:44.277378+00:00", "modified": "2026-03-25T03:05:44.666214+00:00", "id": 19212492, "conceptrecid": "19212491", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.19212492", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.19212491", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.19212492", "metadata": {"title": "Architectural Response to Nippon Life v. OpenAI: Runtime Admissibility as the Missing Layer", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.19212492", "publication_date": "2026-03-25", "description": "<p>This paper responds to Eran Kahana&rsquo;s March 2026 analysis of Nippon Life v. OpenAI by arguing that the case identifies a genuine architectural defect in current large language model deployment. The defect is not merely erroneous output. It is the absence of a mechanism to determine whether model-generated output is admissible before it becomes consequence.</p>\n<p>The paper argues that this defect has a structural answer: runtime admissibility enforcement at inference time. A governance layer positioned between model generation and downstream action can determine whether an output may proceed, must be refused, or requires clarification. In this framework, refusal is a correct system outcome rather than a failure.</p>\n<p>The paper further argues that this architectural answer existed in dated prior work before the present litigation brought the issue into sharper public view.</p>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Stokes, Margaret", "affiliation": null}], "keywords": ["Eran Kahana", "Nippon Life v. OpenAI", "product liability architectural design defect", "runtime admissibility", "inference-time governance", "AI governance", "large language models", "epistemic legitimacy", "legal AI refusal as a system outcome"], "resource_type": {"title": "Publication", "type": "publication"}, "license": {"id": "cc-by-4.0"}, "relations": {"version": [{"index": 0, "is_last": true, "parent": {"pid_type": "recid", "pid_value": "19212491"}}]}}, "title": "Architectural Response to Nippon Life v. OpenAI: 